       "India is described as a 'Union of States' — in Article 1 of the Constitution.",
       "Indian federalism is 'quasi-federal' — it has both federal and unitary features.",
       "Federal features: dual polity (Union and states), a written Constitution, division of powers, an independent judiciary, and bicameralism.",
       "The Seventh Schedule divides powers into three lists — the Union List, the State List and the Concurrent List.",
       "The Union List has about 100 subjects — defence, foreign affairs, railways, banking; only Parliament can legislate on these.",
       "The State List has about 61 subjects — police, public health, agriculture, land; state legislatures legislate on these.",
       "The Concurrent List has about 52 subjects — education, forests, marriage; both can legislate, but in case of conflict the Union law prevails.",
       "Residuary powers rest with the Union — Article 248 (e.g. laws on cyber crime).",
       "Example of a strong Centre: Article 356 — President's Rule can be imposed when constitutional machinery breaks down in a state.",
